Chlorophyll Orbit

Creative Catalyst: The genre was birthed when rogue botanists began translating the fluctuating bio-electrical impulses of rainforest flora into voltage-controlled oscillator signals.

Chlorophyll Orbit sounds like a terrarium left on a spaceship dashboard. It marries the angular, jittery urgency of 1970s new wave with the repetitive, hypnotic thump of acid house, all bathed in a shimmering, celestial reverb. The music feels humid, precise, and vast—as if the Prophet-5 is being played by a machine that is slowly learning how to photosynthesize.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 122–128 BPM; a rigid, machine-driven floor-fill pulse softened by erratic, organic "bio-swing" micro-adjustments.
*   Harmonic Palette: Mixolydian and Dorian modes, favoring bright, major-seventh suspensions that resolve into dissonant, acidic minor-second clusters.
*   Production Signatures: 1) Tape-saturated analog sequencing; 2) Heavy use of resonant filter sweeps modulated by plant-sensor voltage envelopes; 3) "Canopy-panning," where melodic lines drift slowly between the left and right channels to mimic wind through leaves.
*   Dynamics: A slow, creeping build-up that mimics the growth cycle of a vine, culminating in explosive, high-resonance synthesizer squelch-peaks that suddenly collapse into atmospheric silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement coalesced in 1979 in the humid basements of the Amazonian Research Institute in Manaus. A collective of disgraced botanists and synth-geeks, weary of academia, spent their nights wiring sensitive electrodes to Monstera deliciosa and Ficus specimens, routing the resulting data into banks of Sequential Circuits Prophet-5s. They adopted a "lab-chic" aesthetic—white linen jumpsuits stained with chlorophyll, wide-brimmed sun hats, and vintage aviation goggles. Their rituals were held in indoor greenhouses at 3:00 AM, where the music was said to accelerate the growth of the plants, creating a feedback loop between the dancers, the rhythm, and the living room flora.

Instruments: Sequential Circuits Prophet-5 (the heart of the melody), Roland TB-303 (for acidic leaf-pulse bass), custom bio-signal interface modules, and vintage 1970s drum machines.

For Fans Of: Can, Yellow Magic Orchestra, Laurie Spiegel, and early Tangerine Dream.

